        <text x="0" y="78" font-family="'Manrope', sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#36c7b5" text-anchor="middle">Wall Street • East Coast</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     TIME DIFFERENCE & METHOD CARDS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section content-section" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
  <h2 id="method-heading">How to Convert Central Africa Time to EST</h2>
  <p class="method-intro">
    Calculating times between Central Africa and North America requires subtracting 7 hours during standard winter months. Here are three straightforward methods to quickly convert CAT to EST.
  </p>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 1</p>
      <h3>Direct 7-Hour Subtraction</h3>
      <p>
        Subtract 7 hours from the CAT clock. For example, if a business call is scheduled for 3:00 PM CAT in Harare, subtract 7 hours to get 8:00 AM EST in New York.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 2</p>
      <h3>The Overnight Date Rollback Rule</h3>
      <p>
        When converting early morning CAT times (between 12:00 AM and 6:59 AM), subtracting 7 hours rolls back to the late afternoon or evening of the previous day in EST (e.g., 2:00 AM CAT Tuesday = 7:00 PM EST Monday).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 3</p>
      <h3>North American Summer Adjustment (EDT)</h3>
      <p>
        From mid-March to early November, the US East Coast uses Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC-4). During this daylight saving period, subtract only 6 hours from CAT instead of 7 hours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

