<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="where-used-heading">
  <h2 id="where-used-heading">Where CAT & EST Are Used</h2>
  
  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">1. Central & Southern African Nations (CAT)</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Central Africa Time (UTC+2) is officially observed across Mozambique, Zimbabwe, Zambia, Malawi, Rwanda, Burundi, Botswana, and eastern provinces of the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Major commercial cities like Maputo, Harare, Lusaka, and Kigali rely on CAT for commerce, government operations, and education.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">2. US East Coast & Eastern Canada (EST)</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Eastern Standard Time (UTC-5) covers 23 US states in whole or part, including New York, Florida, Pennsylvania, Georgia, Massachusetts, and the District of Columbia. It is also used across Eastern Canadian provinces such as Ontario and Quebec.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">3. Mining & Commodity Supply Chains</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Zambia and the Democratic Republic of the Congo are major global producers of copper and cobalt. Mining headquarters in North America monitor daily production reports and shipment dispatches coming out of Central Africa during US morning hours.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">4. International Development & Global Health NGOs</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Kigali (Rwanda) and Lilongwe (Malawi) host regional headquarters for international development agencies, global health organizations, and humanitarian funds. Communication with program sponsors in Washington D.C. relies heavily on daily afternoon CAT overlap windows.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">5. Energy & Hydroelectric Infrastructure Projects</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Major energy projects such as Kariba Dam (Zambia/Zimbabwe) and Cahora Bassa (Mozambique) involve international engineering consortia based on the US East Coast. Daily operational logs and engineering syncs require aligning CAT workdays with US morning shifts.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">6. Conservation & Wildlife Research Partnerships</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Environmental research centers in national parks like Nyungwe (Rwanda) and South Luangwa (Zambia) share ecological data and satellite tracking logs with US university research departments in EST.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON MISTAKES
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON MISTAKES SECTION
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Mistakes When Converting CAT to EST</h2>
  
  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 1: Forgetting North American Daylight Saving Time</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Central Africa Time remains at UTC+2 all year, but New York switches to EDT (UTC-4) in March. Forgetting this transition results in meeting invitations being off by one hour between March and November.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 2: Confusing CAT with East Africa Time (EAT)</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Neighbouring East African countries (Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, Ethiopia) run on EAT (UTC+3), which is one hour ahead of CAT (UTC+2). Ensure you select CAT (UTC+2) for countries like Zambia, Zimbabwe, or Rwanda.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 3: Date Line Confusion for Early Morning Hours</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      A timestamp of 3:00 AM CAT on Tuesday morning equates to 8:00 PM EST on Monday evening. Miscalculating the date shift can lead to missing overnight automated deployments or flight arrivals.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

